1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,328
Sunday, twenty past nine.

2
00:00:02,378 --> 00:00:07,344
The buyer at the bus stop presses Confirm, and the screen freezes for a second.

3
00:00:07,394 --> 00:00:09,216
So the buyer presses again.

4
00:00:09,266 --> 00:00:15,936
Two presses, one bunch of tulips, and one card that must be charged exactly once.

5
00:00:15,986 --> 00:00:28,536
In Lesson 26, the page took a token from the card frame and ecom checked that it belongs to this payment, and the fee from Lesson 22 and the terminal from Lesson 23 are ready too.

6
00:00:28,586 --> 00:00:32,160
This lesson charges the card, exactly once.

7
00:00:32,210 --> 00:00:34,848
A double press is only the start.

8
00:00:34,898 --> 00:00:37,848
The page can retry on a weak network.

9
00:00:37,898 --> 00:00:42,024
Two copies of the bank's app can pick up the same confirmed card.

10
00:00:42,074 --> 00:00:44,328
Each of them could charge the card.

11
00:00:44,378 --> 00:00:46,080
And there is a worse case.

12
00:00:46,130 --> 00:00:51,864
The app asks the network to charge the card, and crashes before it saves the answer.

13
00:00:51,914 --> 00:00:53,736
Did money move?

14
00:00:53,786 --> 00:00:55,608
Nobody knows.

15
00:00:55,658 --> 00:01:02,312
How do you charge a card exactly once, even when the app crashes?

16
00:01:02,362 --> 00:01:06,008
Here is my rule for any code that moves money.

17
00:01:06,058 --> 00:01:10,184
Decide who goes, write it down, and only then act.

18
00:01:10,234 --> 00:01:12,056
First comes the claim.

19
00:01:12,106 --> 00:01:17,504
The claim is a single change to the payment, from card confirmed to debiting.

20
00:01:17,554 --> 00:01:21,560
It succeeds only if the payment is still card confirmed.

21
00:01:21,610 --> 00:01:27,656
So however many presses or retries arrive, exactly one caller wins.

22
00:01:27,706 --> 00:01:31,400
The others find the payment already taken, and stop.

23
00:01:31,450 --> 00:01:33,896
The winner then looks for two things.

24
00:01:33,946 --> 00:01:37,160
It finds the website's live terminal for this card.

25
00:01:37,210 --> 00:01:40,448
And it finds the fee the bank agreed with Malika.

26
00:01:40,498 --> 00:01:45,872
If either is missing, the payment fails right there, and nothing is charged.

27
00:01:45,922 --> 00:01:49,952
Next, the winner writes a line in the operation journal.

28
00:01:50,002 --> 00:01:53,672
That is the bank's logbook of every call that moves money.

29
00:01:53,722 --> 00:01:59,888
The line says debit, requested, for this payment, through this terminal.

30
00:01:59,938 --> 00:02:03,056
It is written before any network is asked.

31
00:02:03,106 --> 00:02:06,584
And a payment can hold only one debit line.

32
00:02:06,634 --> 00:02:10,928
Then ecom asks the card zone to charge the card by its token.

33
00:02:10,978 --> 00:02:13,064
The call carries its own id.

34
00:02:13,114 --> 00:02:19,760
If it is sent twice, the card zone sees the same id, and the charge still happens once.

35
00:02:19,810 --> 00:02:25,328
Timur's card zone talks to the card processing centre, which asks the buyer's bank.

36
00:02:25,378 --> 00:02:28,352
Some networks only hold the money at first.

37
00:02:28,402 --> 00:02:32,720
For those, the card zone takes the held money at once.

38
00:02:32,770 --> 00:02:35,288
That second step is the capture.

39
00:02:35,338 --> 00:02:39,968
So for every network, approved means the money is taken.

40
00:02:40,018 --> 00:02:41,960
Three answers can come back.

41
00:02:42,010 --> 00:02:45,440
Approved means paid, with a reference number.

42
00:02:45,490 --> 00:02:49,616
Declined is final, with a reason the bank can explain.

43
00:02:49,666 --> 00:02:52,472
And no answer in time is unknown.

44
00:02:52,522 --> 00:02:55,496
Unknown is never treated as declined.

45
00:02:55,546 --> 00:02:59,048
A paid payment never becomes unpaid again.

46
00:02:59,098 --> 00:03:06,296
Back at the bus stop, the page changes to taking the payment, and says the buyer's bank is being asked.

47
00:03:06,346 --> 00:03:11,432
A few seconds later, it says paid, and shows the reference number.

48
00:03:11,482 --> 00:03:14,816
Another buyer tries a card with too little money.

49
00:03:14,866 --> 00:03:21,464
The page says the payment was declined, and gives the reason: there isn't enough money on the card.

50
00:03:21,514 --> 00:03:25,208
That evening, Malika opens the merchant portal.

51
00:03:25,258 --> 00:03:28,568
The payments list shows the tulips as paid.

52
00:03:28,618 --> 00:03:30,440
She opens the payment.

53
00:03:30,490 --> 00:03:34,424
The details show the amount, the website and the bank fee.

54
00:03:34,474 --> 00:03:38,048
Below them is a section called calls to the card scheme.

55
00:03:38,098 --> 00:03:42,224
It has one line: debit, first sent, now approved.

56
00:03:42,274 --> 00:03:46,064
Two presses at the bus stop, and still only one line.

57
00:03:46,114 --> 00:03:49,784
Take away any step, and something breaks.

58
00:03:49,834 --> 00:03:53,864
Without the claim, a double press is a double charge.

59
00:03:53,914 --> 00:04:00,224
Without the journal written first, a crash leaves money moved and no record of it.

60
00:04:00,274 --> 00:04:05,288
Without the call's own id, a retried call charges the card again.

61
00:04:05,338 --> 00:04:13,904
And without unknown, a slow network would tell the buyer to pay again, while the first charge quietly went through.

62
00:04:13,954 --> 00:04:16,064
The buyer is charged once.

63
00:04:16,114 --> 00:04:18,368
Malika is paid once.

64
00:04:18,418 --> 00:04:21,920
And the bank can always say what it asked, and when.

65
00:04:21,970 --> 00:04:30,152
To recap: claim first, write the logbook line, then charge, and unknown is never declined.

66
00:04:30,202 --> 00:04:35,344
So what stops a double press from charging twice?

67
00:04:35,394 --> 00:04:38,344
The claim: only one caller wins.

68
00:04:38,394 --> 00:04:41,872
Next: what happens when the network goes silent.
